From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 21:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qdsp6: q6dsp-lpass-clocks: publish drvdata before
 registering the clocks

q6dsp_clock_dev_probe() calls dev_set_drvdata() *after* the loop that
registers every clock, but the clk ops resolve their container with
dev_get_drvdata(clk->dev):

	static int clk_q6dsp_prepare(struct clk_hw *hw)
	{
		struct q6dsp_clk *clk = to_q6dsp_clk(hw);
		struct q6dsp_cc *cc = dev_get_drvdata(clk->dev);

		return cc->desc->lpass_set_clk(...);
	}

That is safe only as long as nothing prepares a clock while it is still
being registered. The clock framework does exactly that when a clock of
the same name was previously unregistered while still prepared: the
orphan left behind is reparented onto the clock being registered, and
__clk_set_parent_before() replays the child's prepare count onto the new
parent from inside __clk_register().

An ADSP subsystem restart produces precisely that state. The LPASS
clocks go away with consumers still holding them:

	clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_DCODEC
	clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_CLK_ID_TX_CORE_NPL_MCLK
	clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_CLK_ID_TX_CORE_MCLK

and when the PD-restart notifier re-probes q6afe, the first
devm_clk_hw_register() adopts one of those orphans and prepares it
before drvdata has been set, on a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375):

	Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 0000000000000348
	pc : clk_q6dsp_prepare+0x24/0x3c [snd_q6dsp_common]
	lr : clk_core_prepare+0xb8/0x128
	Call trace:
	 clk_q6dsp_prepare+0x24/0x3c [snd_q6dsp_common] (P)
	 clk_core_prepare+0xb8/0x128
	 clk_core_prepare_lock+0x74/0xfc
	 __clk_set_parent_before+0x80/0x230
	 clk_core_reparent_orphans_nolock+0x74/0xc4
	 __clk_register+0x6dc/0xb0c
	 devm_clk_hw_register+0x78/0xc0
	 q6dsp_clock_dev_probe+0x104/0x184 [snd_q6dsp_common]
	 q6afe_probe+0x7c/0x94 [q6afe]
	 apr_device_probe+0x20/0x3c [apr]
	 ...
	 of_register_apr_devices+0x244/0x2a4 [apr]
	 apr_pd_status+0x2c/0x4c [apr]
	 pdr_notifier_work+0xa0/0x1fc [pdr_interface]

0x348 is offsetof(struct q6dsp_cc, desc) (8 + 104 * 8), i.e. cc is NULL.

The oops is worse than a lost audio path, because __clk_register() runs
under the global clk prepare_lock. The worker dies holding it, nothing
ever releases it, and every subsequent clk_prepare_enable() in the system
blocks forever. On this device the visible symptom is that the display
never comes back after a suspend/resume that restarted the ADSP: the DSI
and DPU clocks can no longer be enabled, while anything that does not
touch the clock framework keeps running normally.

Set the drvdata before registering anything. It is the container the ops
already expect, it is fully initialised at that point, and nothing else
uses this device's drvdata.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
 sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6dsp-lpass-clocks.c | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6dsp-lpass-clocks.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6dsp-lpass-clocks.c
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6dsp-lpass-clocks.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6dsp-lpass-clocks.c
@@ -146,6 +146,18 @@
 	cc->dev = dev;
 	q6dsp_clks = desc->clks;
 
+	/*
+	 * The clk ops below resolve their container through the device's
+	 * drvdata, and the clock framework can invoke .prepare() from inside
+	 * devm_clk_hw_register(): if a clock of the same name was previously
+	 * unregistered while still prepared -- which is exactly what an ADSP
+	 * subsystem restart does to the LPASS clocks -- the orphan left behind
+	 * is reparented onto the clock being registered and its prepare count
+	 * is replayed there and then. Publish the drvdata before registering
+	 * anything, or that first .prepare() dereferences a NULL q6dsp_cc.
+	 */
+	dev_set_drvdata(dev, cc);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < desc->num_clks; i++) {
 		unsigned int id = q6dsp_clks[i].clk_id;
 		struct clk_init_data init = {
@@ -178,8 +190,6 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	dev_set_drvdata(dev, cc);
-
 	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(q6dsp_clock_dev_probe);
